  <article class="post">
    <h1>Measurement</h1>
    <div class="it-meta"><span class="pill pub">&#9679; published</span><span>last tended Aug 30, 2026</span><span class="tag">Quantum</span><span>8 min</span></div>

    <h2><span class="qnum">Q1</span>What is it?</h2>
    <p>Measurement is where a quantum computation finally answers a question. A qubit sits in its state,</p>
    <div class="formula">|&psi;&#10217; = &alpha;|0&#10217; + &beta;|1&#10217;</div>
    <p>and the state is amplitudes, not a value. Ask it &ldquo;0 or 1?&rdquo; &mdash; a measurement in the computational basis &#123;|0&#10217;, |1&#10217;&#125; &mdash; and the <strong>Born rule</strong> gives the odds:</p>
    <div class="formula">P(0) = |&alpha;|&sup2; <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;and&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> P(1) = |&beta;|&sup2; <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;with&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> |&alpha;|&sup2; + |&beta;|&sup2; = 1</div>
    <p>That is the bridge the whole field crosses: <strong>quantum state &#10230; classical outcome</strong>. But measurement is more than reading a value off a register, in two ways. The outcome is only <em>probabilistic</em> &mdash; the state fixes the odds, not the answer. And the measurement <strong>changes the state</strong>. If the outcome is 0, the state is now |0&#10217;; if 1, it is |1&#10217;. Ask again in the same basis and you get the same answer with certainty &mdash; the state was updated to match its own readout. This is what gets called <strong>collapse</strong>, and it makes measurement an <em>action</em>, not an observation:</p>
    <div class="formula">prepare &rarr; measure &rarr; state changes</div>
    <blockquote>&ldquo;Measurement just reveals the value the qubit already had&rdquo; &mdash; the coin-flip model again, and it fails here for the same reason it failed the two-Hadamard test: before the measurement there <em>was</em> no value, only amplitudes. The value comes into existence with the outcome.</blockquote>

