    <h2 style="margin-top:40px"><span class="qnum">Q4</span>Where is it used?</h2>
    <p>Every quantum computation ends in one. The conceptual shape of the whole pipeline is:</p>
    <div class="formula">prepare &rarr; apply gates &rarr; interference &rarr; measure &rarr; classical information</div>
    <p>The quantum part of the machine manipulates amplitudes; measurement is the only door those amplitudes can leave through, and it is a narrow door &mdash; one shot, one outcome, state disturbed. Three places where that narrowness turns from nuisance into structure. In <strong>error correction</strong>, syndrome measurements are engineered to ask about the <em>noise</em> without asking about the <em>data</em> &mdash; collapse aimed carefully, so the encoded state survives its own diagnosis. In <strong>quantum cryptography</strong>, BB84 turns collapse into a security guarantee: an eavesdropper must measure, a measurement in the wrong basis disturbs the state, and the disturbance is detectable &mdash; the &ldquo;flaw&rdquo; of measurement doing the protocol's work. And on <strong>real hardware</strong>, readout is a physical process with its own error rates &mdash; often worse than the gate errors &mdash; which is why measurement fidelity sits on every spec sheet and why characterizing readout error is its own discipline.</p>
    <p>To make it concrete: in Qiskit, prepare |+&#10217; and measure &mdash; a fair coin, run after run. Now insert one H before the measurement and run it again: all zeros. Two circuits, one gate apart, opposite statistics &mdash; the cheapest possible demonstration that the measurement you choose is half the physics.</p>

    <h2 class="sec">My open questions</h2>
    <ul class="reading">
      <li>What exactly does &ldquo;collapse&rdquo; mean physically &mdash; a process in the world, or an update to my description of it?<span class="src">&mdash; chasing</span></li>
      <li>How should I think about measurement when the state is mixed &mdash; and on entangled systems, where measuring one qubit changes what I can say about another?<span class="src">&mdash; next nodes</span></li>
      <li>What is the cleanest intuition for POVMs &mdash; what does a non-projective measurement <em>look like</em> physically?<span class="src">&mdash; open</span></li>
      <li>How does tomography actually reconstruct a state from measurement statistics &mdash; and how many measurements does it take?<span class="src">&mdash; bridges to benchmarking</span></li>
      <li>How does measurement error enter real hardware, and how is it separated from gate error?<span class="src">&mdash; bridges to the noise map</span></li>
    </ul>

